/**
 * WeLease Pipeline — ITEM STATUS  v1.0.0 · 2026-08-06
 * Requires Core.gs, Shared.gs.
 *
 * Makes the dashboard writable. The team sets a status and a follow-up date on any row,
 * and the item behaves accordingly until that date arrives.
 *
 * THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES: a work order open 20 days looks identical whether nobody has
 * touched it or a plumber found rot behind the wall and it is genuinely mid-repair. The
 * first needs chasing; the second needs leaving alone until Thursday. Without somewhere
 * to record that, managers re-litigate the same rows every morning and stop trusting the
 * red counts.
 *
 * HOW IT STAYS FAST: the heavy list rendering is still pre-rendered nightly and served
 * from cache. Only this thin status layer is fetched live on page load — a few hundred
 * rows — and applied over the top. Cached speed, live state.
 *
 * WHAT A STATUS DOES:
 *   - the row is tinted and shows who set it, when, and why
 *   - while the follow-up date is in the FUTURE the item leaves the "need action now"
 *     count. It stays visible; it is being handled, not ignored.
 *   - when the follow-up date ARRIVES the item returns to urgent automatically and
 *     appears on the Follow-ups due list
 *   - Done hides the row until the underlying condition recurs
 *
 * Nothing here writes to AppFolio. A status is how WeLease is handling the item, not a
 * change to the record.
 */

var STATUS_TAB = 'Item_Status';

/**
 * The vocabulary. Deliberately short — a long list means nobody picks consistently and
 * the field stops meaning anything.
 *
 * `urgentWhenDue` marks the statuses where the follow-up date is a real commitment.
 * "Done" carries no commitment; "Waiting on vendor" very much does.
 */
/**
 * TWO AXES, NOT ONE.
 *
 * A single status list is either too long to pick from consistently or too generic to
 * describe the work. Worse, it hides a distinction that matters enormously:
 *
 *   "Done"             this instance is handled. If the condition recurs — the work
 *                      order ages again, a new deposit passes 21 days — it SHOULD come
 *                      back. That is the system working.
 *   "Never show again" this specific item is permanently suppressed. Correct for a
 *                      known exception; catastrophic if applied by accident, because
 *                      the row disappears and nobody remembers why.
 *
 * Conflating those is how a worklist quietly stops being trustworthy. So:
 *
 *   AXIS 1 — DISPOSITION   what is happening, in the vocabulary of THAT work
 *   AXIS 2 — PERSISTENCE   how long it sticks
 *
 * Persistence is chosen for you by the disposition, and can be overridden. Permanent
 * suppression requires a note and is listed in its own auditable register — anything
 * that makes work vanish has to be reversible and visible.
 */

var PERSISTENCE = {
  FOLLOW_UP: 'follow_up',   // returns on the follow-up date
  THIS_CYCLE: 'this_cycle', // hidden until the underlying condition recurs
  PERMANENT: 'permanent'    // never again for this item, until someone deletes the row
};

